





1. Store Field Values

Every cell in the lattice holds the local values of:

warp_field

grav_field

stress_field

charge_field

Each cell is like a tiny “spacetime voxel.”

2. Provide Spatial Access

The lattice lets the engine:

read a cell

write a cell

update a region

scan a neighborhood

This is how the FieldDynamicsEngine evolves the simulation.

3. Snapshot the Entire State

At any moment, the lattice can output a full snapshot:

for visualization

for debugging

for crash analysis

for regime mapping

This is how you get warp‑field holograms, grav‑maps, stress‑heatmaps, etc.

4. Define Simulation Resolution

The lattice controls:

how detailed the simulation is

how smooth the fields evolve

how expensive the computation becomes

Higher resolution = more detail, more cost.Lower resolution = faster, less detail.

You choose the balance.

🜃 Why StateLattice Matters

Without the lattice:

fields have nowhere to exist

organisms have no environment

dynamics have no space to evolve

regimes have no data to evaluate

crashes have no signatures to detect

It is the foundation of the entire Emphemsa simulation.

Everything else plugs into it.

🜄 How It Fits Into the Engine Flow

OntologyKernel → builds organism + fields
StateLattice   → creates the simulation space
FieldDynamics  → evolves fields inside the lattice
RegimeOrch     → reads lattice to determine regime
Permissions    → clamps or shuts down based on lattice values
CrashAnalyzer  → detects collapse from lattice patterns

The lattice is the central data structure every module depends on.



